+## `superclaude reflect contract-status`
+
+### What it does
+
+Reports whether the `/sc:pr-submit` review-detection contract is ready to use.
+It does not launch any reflect audit, start a `claude` subprocess, or touch a
+tasklist.
+
+### Syntax
+
+```bash
+superclaude reflect contract-status [--validate] [--repo OWNER/NAME] [--pr N]
+```
+
+- `--validate` — validate the existing file-based evidence before reporting.
+  This writes a validation report next to the evidence it checked.
+- `--repo OWNER/NAME` — the repository to compare against the validation
+  metadata.
+- `--pr N` — the pull request number to compare against the validation metadata.
+
+### Output
+
+The command prints plain `key: value` lines, including:
+
+- `state:` — one of `missing`, `unlocked`, `unparseable`, `evidence_missing`,
+  `validation_missing`, `validation_failed`, `stale`, `ready` or
+  `declined_by_user`.
+- `checked_paths:` — the files it looked at.
+- `blocker_count:` (and a `blockers:` list when there are any).
+- `next_command:` — what to run next. When the state is `ready` it suggests
+  `/sc:pr-submit --monitor 3 --pr <N>`; for `validation_missing`,
+  `validation_failed`, `stale`, `unlocked` or `evidence_missing` it suggests
+  the same `contract-status` command with `--validate`.
+
+```bash
+# Check readiness for PR 123 on the fork
+superclaude reflect contract-status --repo IronbellyOrg/IronClaude --pr 123
+
+# Validate the evidence first, then report
+superclaude reflect contract-status --validate --repo IronbellyOrg/IronClaude --pr 123
+```
+
+If `SUPERCLAUDE_REFLECT_WRAPPER_ACTIVE=1` is set in your shell, this command
+exits `0` at once without printing a report (see section 5.1).
+
